WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:04.565
Public-key cryptography allows parties to communicate or verify trust without first

00:00:04.565 --> 00:00:06.600
sharing a secret key through a private channel.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.575
RSA, Diffie-Hellman, and elliptic curve cryptography are common examples.

00:00:10.575 --> 00:00:15.500
They appear in key exchange, certificates, digital signatures, secure shell access, VPNs,

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:18.690
TLS, identity systems, code signing, firmware signing,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.700
and many forms of public key infrastructure.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:24.380
The risk is serious because these mechanisms protect more than confidentiality.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.320
Key exchange helps parties establish session keys for encrypted communication.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.450
Digital signatures support integrity, authenticity, and trust.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.975
Certificates bind identities to public keys.

00:00:33.975 --> 00:00:36.795
PKI connects those certificates into chains of trust.

00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:40.310
If public-key assumptions fail, the impact can reach authentication,

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.000
software supply chain trust, device identity, and secure update workflows.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:44.680
TLS, VPNs,

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.700
and SSH are easy examples because they are visible to administrators and security teams.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.930
But public-key cryptography also appears inside application frameworks, service meshes,

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:57.450
cloud control planes, package managers, mobile apps, hardware devices,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.700
and vendor products.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.400
Many teams do not have a complete map of these dependencies,

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:04.800
which is why cryptographic inventory is a foundational activity.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.595
This course avoids instructions for attacking cryptography.

00:01:07.595 --> 00:01:09.960
The practitioner focus is defensive readiness.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.845
The useful questions are where public-key cryptography exists,

00:01:12.845 --> 00:01:16.000
what data or trust depends on it, who controls the implementation,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:17.395
how long it must remain secure,

00:01:17.395 --> 00:01:21.570
and what would be required to migrate it without breaking availability, interoperability,

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:23.400
compliance, or operational trust.
